commentBody:
I've been up half the night; I really did not feel in the mood for a chase around the grid ("With 56-Across," etc.) and names of rock bands and comic book-based characters (except for Tweetie-Bird, maybe)....

Other "NOT GOOD SIGNS:"
"Handicapped entrance off alley" (or worse, around the block);
"Picture showing, but heat not working" (We went ahead, lived to regret the decision);
"Nur fűr Schwindelfrei"  (Fortunately, I translated that one for our group....

@Heidi
This is like the moment in which the pallid and deathly ill heroine's eyes flutter and open, as the background music changes to a major key.
You are right to be proud.

@JohnWM 

If they're temporary, they're probably going to be routing traffic unexpected ways if it's a road you travel frequently. It's a good thing to be aware of if, say, the usual three lanes have been reduced to two, with all the expected merging and slowing down, because of workers in one of the lanes.

Not a good time to be changing stations on your rsdio while glancing in the rear view mirror.

@Dick Yates

The cross at BULGARI and HAIM was my personal natick. I am not exactly in the market for luxury brands, and I stopped listening to most pop music about thirty years ago, but the "A" was an easy guess. No lookups, not even to check my spelling...

But somehow, what went on with the puzzle isn't affecting me much at the moment.

@Grant

 Well, for the Romans, chisling a "V" into stone was a lot easier than chisling a "U," so they made that substitution a lot.

Doing it in print via a computer keyboard so as to make your logo seem more "Roman" is either sly and witty or pretentious, depending on which side of the duality-of-all-things coin you choose to favor.
